// Water where the waves are really there.
//
// Every renderer can make a flat plane look wet.  Tilt its normals about with a `normal` block and,
// looked at from above, the thing is convincing: the light breaks up, the reflections wobble, it reads
// as water.  Then move the camera down to where a seascape actually looks -- along the surface, out
// toward the horizon -- and it falls apart, because a plane with tilted normals is still a plane and
// its horizon is still a ruled line.
//
// So the water here is an **isosurface**: a function of position whose zero is the surface, marched
// until a ray crosses it.  The waves are geometry.  The horizon is ragged because there are wave
// crests standing in front of other wave crests, the near troughs really are below the near crests,
// and every reflection is off the slope the water actually has at that point.
//
// **The sea state is one word**, from the `water` library, which holds sea states the way `daylight`
// holds skies: by name, because the numbers that make water look right -- wavelength, crest exponent
// -- are the ones nobody can guess.  This is `choppy`; the library runs from `glassy` to `rough`.
//
// **On how far the water reaches.**  It runs two thousand units off, which is far past anything the
// picture can resolve, and that is on purpose: the box costs almost nothing to enlarge, and one cut
// short ends in a straight edge lying in mid-water.  A first attempt put three seas side by side to
// compare states and got neither -- from a camera down at the surface, where a seascape belongs, the
// two outer seas were off the edges of the frame and their near boundaries drew a hard line across
// the water.  Comparisons belong in the documentation; a picture is better off being one sea.
//
// **On what the water is showing.**  Water is mostly a mirror, so most of what is in this picture
// arrives from the sky rather than from the sea, which is why the sky is a graded one and not a flat
// color -- and why it is bright near the horizon, since that pale band is what the far water hands
// back.  Note which way round the two halves of the water read: the near water is *dark*, because
// looking down into water is looking through it, and there is nothing under an open sea but more
// water.  The far water is bright, because along the surface it is a mirror.  Nothing arranges that;
// it falls out of the angle, and it is the same effect that makes a wet road shine ahead of you and
// not at your feet.  A mirror in an empty room shows an empty room, and flat-lit water looks like paint.  The
// posts are here for the same reason: something has to stand in the water for its reflection to break
// across the waves, and a reflection that bends over a crest is worth more than any amount of detail
// in the water itself.
//
// **On the cost, which is the honest price of real waves.**  Detail at a distance aliases: at one
// sample a pixel the far water turns to grey mush, since each pixel is averaging normals that mirror
// the bright sky against normals that look down into the dark, and the mean of those is neither. This
// renders with anti-aliasing for that reason and for no other.

import 'water' { Water, SeaWater }

context {
    parallel pixel scanner
    angles are degrees
    no gamma
    info {
        title 'A Sea With Weather In It'
        author 'J. Stephen Kress'
        description 'Three sea states, one word apart, with the waves as real geometry.'
        copyright true
        software __software__
    }
}

// Down at the surface, which is where a seascape belongs and also where the argument is: from here
// the crests stand against the sky and the horizon is ragged because there are waves in front of
// waves.  A flat plane with tilted normals cannot do that at any camera height, and this is the height
// at which it stops being able to pretend.
camera {
    location [0, 1.5, -17]
    look at [0, 1.35, 44]
    field of view 54
}

point light {
    location [-90, 26, 30]
    color [1.55, 1.46, 1.30]
}

// A graded sky.  The pigment is painted on a unit sphere infinitely far off, so its own scale means
// nothing here and the stops run over the whole dome.
background linear Y gradient {
    [0.0, [1.05, 1.06, 1.02],
     0.30, [0.78, 0.86, 0.96],
     0.60, [0.48, 0.66, 0.90],
     1.0, [0.16, 0.36, 0.74]]
}

object Water(2600, 2100, 'choppy', 4) { material SeaWater  translate Z 1000 }

// Something to break a reflection over. A worn post reads as scale in a way an unbroken surface never
// can: without it there is nothing in the picture to say whether the swell is a foot high or twenty.
Post = material {
    pigment [0.30, 0.24, 0.18]
    ambient 0.10
    specular 0.16
    shininess 30
}

cylinder {
    material Post
    min Y 0  max Y 1
    scale [0.5, 3.4, 0.5]
    translate [-4.5, -0.7, 22]
}

cylinder {
    material Post
    min Y 0  max Y 1
    scale [0.42, 2.6, 0.42]
    translate [5.5, -0.6, 30]
}

cylinder {
    material Post
    min Y 0  max Y 1
    scale [0.38, 2.1, 0.38]
    translate [12, -0.5, 46]
}
